Not directly; use wrappers.

In Java, Collections cannot store primitive data types directly. They store objects, so we use Wrapper Classes to
represent primitive data types.

Java provides automatic conversion through Autoboxing and Unboxing.
1. Primitive types vs Wrapper classes
Primitive type	Wrapper class
int	Integer
long	Long
double	Double
float	Float
char	Character
boolean	Boolean
short	Short
byte	Byte

2. Example: Collection cannot directly store primitives
List<int> numbers = new ArrayList<>(); // Compilation error


This is invalid because Java generics support reference types, not primitive types.
3. Correct approach using Wrapper classes
import java.util.*;

public class WrapperExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();

        numbers.add(10);
        numbers.add(20);
        numbers.add(30);

        System.out.println(numbers);
    }
}


Here, Integer is the wrapper class for primitive int.
4. Autoboxing and Unboxing
Autoboxing: Automatic conversion from primitive to wrapper object.
int number = 100;

Integer integerObject = number; // Autoboxing


Unboxing: Automatic conversion from wrapper object to primitive.
Integer integerObject = 100;

int number = integerObject; // Unboxing


5. Real-world interview example
List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();

numbers.add(10);  // int → Integer (Autoboxing)
numbers.add(20);
numbers.add(30);

int result = numbers.get(0); // Integer → int (Unboxing)

System.out.println(result); // 10


Interview-ready answer:

"Java Collections work with objects rather than primitive data types. Therefore, we use wrapper classes such as
Integer, Double and Boolean. Java provides autoboxing and unboxing to automatically convert between primitives and
their corresponding wrapper objects."

One additional point for experienced Java developers: Wrapper objects consume more memory than primitive values and
introduce boxing/unboxing overhead. For performance-sensitive numerical operations, primitive arrays such as int[]
can be more efficient than List<Integer>.
